[# Section 3 — surfaces and projections.
   Authored 2026-05-22, expanded with full per-surface detail.
   Repaired 2026-08-20 against the verification audit — every projection
   example re-recorded from the live binary; matrix reproduced from
   spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md §9. #]

[section id=surfaces group='Ring 0 — Data' n=3 title='Surfaces and projections'

  [intro
    """CX has one canonical surface (the bracket form) and projections
       for JSON, YAML, TOML, XML, Markdown, and CSV/TSV/PSV.
       Every projection is defined as a transformation of canonical CX.
       The normative rules live in
       [`spec/03-approved/core/canonical.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/canonical.md) and
       [`spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md); this section
       is the developer-facing working summary."""]

  [child id=cx-canonical n=3.1 title='CX — the canonical dialect'
    [intro
      """CX has **two** canonical forms — one for source-of-truth
         formatting (preserves comments, anchors, presentation), one for
         content-addressing (strips presentation, expands aliases,
         normalizes everything that doesn't change data). Per
         [`spec/03-approved/core/canonical.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/canonical.md) §1."""]

    [child id=canonical-lossless n=3.1.1 title='Lossless canonical (cx fmt)'
      [body
        """Lossless canonical (`cx fmt`) preserves every node: comments,
           anchors, aliases, merges, CX directives, BlockContent,
           RawText, processing instructions. It normalizes only
           **presentation**: whitespace, indent, quoting, number
           formatting."""]
      [list
        [item """**Idempotent:** `fmt(fmt(x)) == fmt(x)` for any valid `x`."""]
        [item """**Use cases:** opinionated source-file formatter; diff
                stability across edits that don't change data; code
                review where reviewers see only meaningful changes."""]]
      [example lang=cx
        """[; Source — author-friendly form (loose layout): ]
           [user
             email="ada@example.com"
             id=42
             active=true]

           [; cx fmt output — presentation normalized, one line: ]
           [user email="ada@example.com" id=42 active=true]"""]]

    [child id=canonical-strict n=3.1.2 title='Strict canonical (cx canonical / cx hash)'
      [body
        """Strict canonical (`cx canonical`) reduces input to
           **data-equivalence form**. Strips comments and directives,
           expands anchors and aliases, resolves merges, normalizes
           datetime offsets to UTC, renames declared ids to a
           deterministic document-order scheme. Two CX documents
           have identical strict canonical bytes if and only if they
           encode the same data. Attribute order is data — it is
           preserved, never sorted."""]
      [list
        [item """**Use cases:** `cx hash` (content-addressable hashing —
                SHA-256 of strict canonical bytes); signed
                configuration (sign the strict canonical bytes;
                verify after canonicalizing); deduplication keyed on
                data, not file bytes."""]
        [item """**Strict canonical is what the identity story rests on.**
                Two documents that "encode the same data" but differ on
                comments, whitespace, quoting, or anchor names
                still hash to the same bytes."""]]
      [example lang=cx
        """[; Source: ]
           [user
             [; regrettably noisy comment ]
             email='ada@example.com'
             id=42
             active=true]

           [; cx canonical output — comment stripped, attribute order
              preserved, bare strings where bare-eligible: ]
           [user email=ada@example.com id=42 active=true]"""]
      [body
        """The strict-canonical bytes are what the SHA-256 hash is
           taken over (see [[hash]]) and what the round-trip guarantee
           is checked against (see [[roundtrip]])."""]]]

  [child id=json n=3.2 title='JSON projection'
    [intro
      """JSON is the most common interchange surface. CX → JSON mapping
         is straightforward; JSON → CX is well-defined for the JSON
         subset that maps cleanly to CX's data model. Per
         [`spec/03-approved/core/canonical.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/canonical.md) §5 and
         [`spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md) §2.2 / §4."""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[user id=42 email='ada@example.com' active=true
              [roles ('admin', 'editor')]]"""]

    [example lang=json
      """{
           "user": {
             "id": 42,
             "email": "ada@example.com",
             "active": true,
             "roles": ["admin", "editor"]
           }
         }"""]

    [body
      """**Basic mapping:** Attributes become object keys; element bodies
         of pure-element content become nested objects keyed by child
         element name; sequences become arrays; scalars map directly."""]

    [child id=json-mixed-content n=3.2.1 title='Mixed content and the lossless $tag envelope'
      [body
        """Mixed content — text interleaved with inline elements — has
           no native JSON form. The **default** projection is lossy: the
           text runs collect into an array under a reserved `"_"` key and
           the inline elements become sibling keys, so run order is not
           recoverable. Under `--lossless`, an element instead emits as
           the reserved `$tag` **envelope**
           ([`spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md) §2.2.1):
           `$children` is an ordered array of runs, so mixed content
           survives exactly."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[para 'The function ' [code 'parse'] ' returns a value.']"""]
      [example lang=json
        """{ "$tag": "para", "$children": ["The function ", {"$tag": "code", "$children": ["parse"]}, " returns a value."] }"""]
      [body
        """JSON → CX reverses this unconditionally: a `$tag` envelope
           reconstructs the element with its body in order. The
           round-trip is byte-identical against `cx canonical` output
           (`cx eq` accepts the recovered document); the envelope also
           carries attributes (`$attrs` + `$attr-types`), element
           metadata (`$anchor` / `$merge` / `$id` / `$type`), and
           `[table]` payloads (`$cols` / `$rows`)."""]]

    [child id=json-namespaces n=3.2.2 title='Namespace handling'
      [body
        """CX namespace declarations (`xmlns:dc=URI`) project as JSON
           object keys with the `xmlns:` prefix preserved. Qualified
           names like `dc:title` project as JSON keys with the colon
           preserved (JSON keys are arbitrary strings, so this is
           legal). Round-trip is lossless when consumers preserve the
           `xmlns:` attributes."""]]

    [child id=json-numbers n=3.2.3 title='Number precision and BigInt'
      [body
        """JSON numbers are IEEE-754 doubles per RFC 8259, with
           ambiguous integer support beyond `2^53-1`. CX exports int64
           integers as JSON numbers; `bigint` scalars export as JSON
           strings, with the type recovered in lossless mode via the
           `cx:type` sidecar (map positions) or the per-item
           `{"cx:bigint": "…"}` carrier (array positions) — see
           [`spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md) §0.2.
           Sized variants (`::i32`, `::u64`) keep their native number
           image; the width name rides the envelope's `$type` /
           `$attr-types` in lossless mode."""]
      [body
        """Floats with `nan` / `inf` / `-inf` are not reachable from
           pure CX arithmetic (CX floats are finite-only); at the FFI
           boundary the JSON module's `nan-handling` option controls
           `null` / string / error behavior."""]]

    [child id=json-null n=3.2.4 title='null vs missing'
      [body
        """JSON has no way to distinguish "key absent" from "key present
           with value null." CX preserves the distinction where JSON can
           express it: `[user email=null]` projects to
           `{"user": {"email": null}}`, while an empty element `[user]`
           projects to `{"user": null}` (an element with no attributes
           and no body has nothing to become an object of). JSON → CX
           reads absent keys as absent attributes (NOT as null)."""]]]

  [child id=yaml n=3.3 title='YAML projection'
    [intro
      """YAML follows the JSON projection but uses YAML's mapping and
         sequence syntax — including the lossless `$tag` envelope, which
         is the same encoding in both lanes
         ([`spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md) §2.3.1):
         CX anchors and merges ride the envelope's `$anchor` / `$merge`
         keys in lossless mode."""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[defaults &server-base host='localhost' tls=true]
         [service-a *server-base port=8001]"""]

    [example lang=yaml
      """$doc:
           - $tag: defaults
             $anchor: server-base
             $attrs:
               host: localhost
               tls: true
           - $tag: service-a
             $merge: server-base
             $attrs:
               port: 8001"""]

    [body
      """YAML's loose schema (untyped scalars, multiple ways to spell
         the same value) is normalized on input: `yes`/`true`/`True`
         all map to canonical `true`, numeric strings stay strings,
         etc. The conversion is documented in
         [`spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md) §5."""]

    [child id=yaml-tags n=3.3.1 title='YAML tags (!!binary and the !!cx: family)'
      [body
        """Two tag families are honored on YAML → CX parse: `!!binary`
           (base64 payload → CX `:bytes` — `a: !!binary aGVsbG8=`
           parses to `[a 0x68656c6c6f]`) and the CX-specific `!!cx:T`
           type carriers. Any other tag — including the standard
           `!!str` / `!!int` — is not interpreted: the **whole scalar
           text, tag included, is kept as one string** (`!!custom foo`
           parses to the string `'!!custom foo'`). On emit, ambiguity
           is prevented by quoting, not tagging: a numeric-looking
           string emits as `'42'`; bytes emit with `!!binary`."""]]

    [child id=yaml-multiline n=3.3.2 title='Multiline scalars (| and >)'
      [body
        """On input, YAML's literal (`|`) and folded (`>`) block styles
           parse per YAML 1.2 — newlines preserved or folded
           respectively. On emit there is **no block-scalar form**:
           the canonical YAML emitter is block-style for structure but
           writes multi-line string values as ordinary quoted scalars
           with escaped newlines (`body: "line one\\nline two"`), which
           round-trip exactly."""]]

    [child id=yaml-anchors n=3.3.3 title='Anchors and aliases'
      [body
        """YAML-native anchors (`&name`) and aliases (`*name`) are
           resolved (expanded) on YAML → CX parse — the data content is
           preserved, the anchor/alias structure is not
           ([`spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md) §5.1).
           In the other direction the default YAML emit is symmetric:
           CX anchors and merges are **resolved** — aliases expand to
           their referent, merge hosts inherit — so the YAML carries the
           semantic document (the Resolved AST, `ast.md`). The lossless
           envelope instead carries the authored structure via `$anchor`
           / `$merge` keys, which the importer reconstructs — so a
           `--lossless` round-trip preserves them."""]]]

  [child id=toml n=3.4 title='TOML projection'
    [intro
      """CX → TOML projects the element tree onto TOML tables: each
         nested element becomes a section whose header is the
         **quoted dotted path** from the root, and repeated
         identically-named children become an array of tables. Per
         [`spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md) §2.4 / §6."""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[config
           [server host='localhost' tls=true]
           [users
             [user id=1 email='ada@example.com']
             [user id=2 email='grace@example.com']]]"""]

    [example lang=toml
      """[config]
         ["config.server"]
         host = "localhost"
         tls = true

         ["config.users"]
         [["config.users.user"]]
         id = 1
         email = "ada@example.com"

         [["config.users.user"]]
         id = 2
         email = "grace@example.com" """]

    [child id=toml-inline n=3.4.1 title='Section headers, not inline tables'
      [body
        """TOML allows three spellings for nested scalars: inline tables
           (`server = { host = "x" }`), dotted keys (`server.host = "x"`),
           and section headers. All three parse; the emitter's choice is
           **structural, not size-based** — there is no entry-count
           threshold. Every nested element emits as a section with a
           quoted dotted-path header (`["config.server"]`), scalars as
           `key = value` lines beneath it."""]]

    [child id=toml-array-of-tables n=3.4.2 title='Array of tables'
      [body
        """`[[users]]` repeated on the TOML side produces an array of
           maps on import (TOML → CX yields map / array values, not
           elements). CX → TOML emits `[["path.to.name"]]` — the
           quoted-dotted-path array-of-tables header — for any sibling
           group of identically-named child elements, preserving
           order; a one-of-a-kind child emits a plain quoted section
           header, never a dotted key."""]]]

  [child id=xml n=3.5 title='XML projection'
    [intro
      """XML is the closest projection — both CX and XML are
         element-with-attributes-and-body languages. The mapping is
         essentially one-to-one for element / attribute / text content.
         Per [`spec/03-approved/core/canonical.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/canonical.md) §8 and
         [`spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md) §2.1 / §3."""]

    [example lang=cx
      '''[user id=42 email='ada@example.com'
           [profile
             [bio """Computing pioneer."""]]]''']

    [example lang=xml
      """<user id="42" email="ada@example.com">
           <profile>
             <bio>Computing pioneer.</bio>
           </profile>
         </user>"""]

    [body
      """Differences from XML: CX has no DTD validation layer (schemas
         are first-class CX, see [[schema]]); namespace declarations
         are CX attributes with the `xmlns:` prefix preserved; CDATA
         sections project to CX raw-text nodes (`[#…#]`), whose
         contents are opaque bytes."""]

    [child id=xml-pi n=3.5.1 title='Processing instructions and comments'
      [body
        """XML processing instructions (`<?xml-stylesheet …?>`) project
           to CX directive elements (`[?xml-stylesheet …]`). XML
           comments (`<!-- … -->`) project to CX comments (`[; … ]`).
           Both round-trip losslessly in the lossless canonical form;
           the strict canonicalizer strips comments per
           [[canonical-strict]]."""]]

    [child id=xml-space-roundtrip n=3.5.2 title='xml:space round-trip'
      [body
        """`xml:space=preserve` survives XML round-trip identically —
           as an **inert pass-through attribute**. CX gives it no
           engine semantics: quoted body text is verbatim anyway (see
           [[xml-space]]), so there is nothing for the attribute to
           switch. It rides along for XML consumers that honor it."""]]

    [child id=xml-doctype-dtd n=3.5.3 title='DOCTYPE and DTD'
      [body
        """CX does not have a DTD layer. XML input with a DOCTYPE
           declaration is accepted; the declaration is captured as a
           **DoctypeDecl prolog node** on the document (`<!DOCTYPE html>`
           imports as `[!DOCTYPE html]` ahead of the root element).
           DTD internal-subset content (`<!ELEMENT>`, `<!ATTLIST>`,
           `<!ENTITY>`) is carried verbatim inside that node and is
           not used for validation — schemas are first-class CX
           (`.cxs` files, see [[schema]])."""]]]

  [child id=md n=3.6 title='Markdown projection'
    [intro
      """Markdown is a **typed-lossy codec**, not one of the lossless
         data lanes ([`spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md) §7).
         MD → CX yields a `[doc]`-rooted prose tree: headings become
         `[h1] [h2] …`; paragraphs become `[p]`; lists become `[ul]` /
         `[ol]` / `[li]`; inline code becomes `[code]`. The MD → CX → MD
         direction is byte-stable for the supported subset, but
         CX ↔ MD in general is lossy — element names, attributes, and
         types outside the prose vocabulary do not survive."""]

    [example lang=md
      """# Hello

         A paragraph with *emphasis* and `code`.

         - item 1
         - item 2"""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[doc
           [h1 Hello]
           [p 'A paragraph with ' [em emphasis] ' and ' [code code] '.']
           [ul
             [li item 1]
             [li item 2]]]"""]

    [child id=md-tables n=3.6.1 title='GFM tables'
      [body
        """A GFM pipe table in Markdown input is **not decomposed** into
           `[tr]` / `[th]` / `[td]` structure — the table's source lines
           are carried as raw text inside a `[table]` element, and
           re-emit reproduces them byte-for-byte."""]
      [example lang=md
        """| Name | Age |
           |:-----|----:|
           | Ada  |  35 |"""]
      [example lang=cx check=none
        """[doc
           [table [#| Name | Age |
         |:-----|----:|
         | Ada  |  35 |#]]]"""]
      [; check=none — illustrative panel: the [# … #] raw-text payload
         reproduces MD-importer output whose leading-pipe lines depend on
         column-0 alignment that the guide's indented panel cannot carry. ]
      [body
        """In the other direction, a CX `[table[…]]` block (see
           [[tables]]) emits as a real GFM pipe table — header row from
           the declared column names, then one row per data row — per
           [`spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md) §7."""]]

    [child id=md-fences n=3.6.2 title='Fenced code with language'
      [body
        """Fenced code blocks (` ```lang ... ``` `) project to a
           `[code]` element with a bare `lang` attribute and the code
           carried verbatim as raw text — `[code lang=python
           [#print(1)#]]`. There is no `[pre]` wrapper. The language
           identifier round-trips."""]]

    [child id=md-frontmatter n=3.6.3 title='Frontmatter'
      [body
        """YAML frontmatter (between `---` markers at top of file)
           projects to **attributes on the `[doc]` root element**.
           Values stay strings — a frontmatter `date:` is not
           auto-typed into a CX date."""]
      [example lang=md
        """---
           title: My Post
           date: 2026-05-22
           ---

           # Body"""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[doc title='My Post' date='2026-05-22'
           [h1 Body]]"""]]

    [child id=md-footnotes n=3.6.4 title='Footnotes'
      [body
        """GFM footnote syntax (`text[^1]` … `[^1]: ref`) is **not
           implemented** — there are no `[fn-ref]` / `[fn-def]`
           elements. Footnote markers are treated as ordinary inline
           text by the prose parser and do not round-trip as
           footnotes. Keep footnotes out of Markdown that must
           survive a CX round-trip."""]]

    [child id=md-math n=3.6.5 title='Math blocks'
      [body
        """Math syntax (`$inline$`, `$$display$$`) is **not
           implemented** — there is no `[math]` element. Dollar-sign
           math is treated as ordinary inline text and is not
           protected from the prose parser's emphasis and pairing
           rules. Keep LaTeX math out of Markdown that must survive
           a CX round-trip."""]]]

  [child id=delimited n=3.7 title='Delimited projection (CSV / TSV / PSV)'
    [intro
      """CSV (comma-separated), TSV (tab-separated), and PSV
         (pipe-separated) all project via the same delimited-data
         pipeline. Per
         [`spec/03-approved/core/canonical.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/canonical.md) §9 and
         [`spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md) §8.
         Delimited round-trip is well-defined but **lossy** (column
         types are not preserved on emit, only on the in-memory
         CX side)."""]

    [example lang=csv
      """id,name,email,active
         1,Ada,ada@example.com,true
         2,Grace,grace@example.com,false"""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[table [table[id::int name email active::bool]]
           1 Ada ada@example.com true
           2 Grace grace@example.com false
         ]"""]

    [child id=delim-header n=3.7.1 title='Header row'
      [body
        """The first row is **always the header** — its cells become
           the column names of the imported `[table[…]]` block. There
           is no header-detection heuristic and no flag to declare the
           header absent; headerless data needs a header row added
           before import."""]]

    [child id=delim-types n=3.7.2 title='Type inference'
      [body
        """Per-column type inference scans the **whole column**: all
           values match int → `::int`; all match int or float →
           `::float`; all bool → `::bool`; all date or datetime →
           `::date` / `::datetime`; otherwise the column stays string
           (the untyped default). One odd value anywhere in the column
           is enough to keep it string — inference never truncates its
           scan."""]]

    [child id=delim-quoting n=3.7.3 title='Quoting and escaping'
      [body
        """RFC 4180 rules: fields containing the delimiter, double
           quote, or newline are wrapped in double quotes; internal
           double quotes are escaped as `""`. Output canonicalizes to
           RFC 4180 regardless of input quoting style. Lone CR / LF
           outside quotes are treated as row terminators; embedded
           CRLF inside a quoted field is preserved verbatim."""]
      [body
        """CSV → CX → CSV is byte-identical for RFC-4180-conformant
           input (quoted fields and escaped quotes included). The
           emit direction is defined only for table-shaped documents:
           a CX document that is not a single flattenable table
           refuses with a named `emit_delimited` error rather than
           guessing a tabular shape."""]]]

  [child id=bytes-wire n=3.8 title='Bytes scalar across surfaces'
    [intro
      """The `:bytes` scalar kind (see [[bytes]]) has a different wire
         form per surface — no surface has a single native binary form
         that all the others share. The CX↔surface bridge applies a
         deterministic encoding per surface:"""]

    [table
      [tr [th 'surface'] [th 'wire form'] [th 'notes']]
      [tr [td 'CX (canonical)'] [td '0x<hex>'] [td 'hex digits, even count; the only literal form']]
      [tr [td 'data-bin'] [td 'length-prefixed raw'] [td 'no encoding overhead']]
      [tr [td 'ast-bin'] [td 'length-prefixed raw'] [td 'same as data-bin']]
      [tr [td 'JSON'] [td 'string (base64)'] [td 'lossless adds the cx:type sidecar; JSON has no native bytes']]
      [tr [td 'YAML'] [td 'string with !!binary tag'] [td 'YAML 1.2 binary tag']]
      [tr [td 'XML'] [td 'hex text + cx:type="bytes"'] [td 'the sidecar attribute is emitted unconditionally, not only under --lossless']]
      [tr [td 'TOML'] [td 'string (base64)'] [td 'TOML has no binary type']]
      [tr [td 'Markdown'] [td 'HTML-comment fallback'] [td 'a bytes-bearing element has no prose image; it emits as an HTML comment']]
      [tr [td 'CSV / TSV'] [td '0x<hex> text in the cell'] [td 'deserializer needs schema to know it is bytes']]
      [tr [td 'CXCol / Parquet'] [td 'binary column type'] [td 'native; zero-copy through the Arrow bridge']]]

    [body
      """The table states the shipped behavior per lane: JSON / TOML
         emit base64 strings, YAML emits `!!binary`, XML emits the hex
         text with the `cx:type="bytes"` sidecar, and the lossless
         JSON lane recovers the bytes type via the `cx:type` sidecar /
         `{"cx:bytes": …}` carrier."""]]

  [child id=roundtrip n=3.9 title='Round-trip guarantees and lossiness matrix'
    [intro
      """The round-trip contract is the single most important property
         of CX's surface design. Stated formally:"""]

    [note
      """For any CX document **D** in canonical form, and any
         **lossless-lane** projection format **F** (XML, or JSON / YAML
         under `--lossless`), the following identity holds:

         `to_cx(to_F(D)) == D` (as canonical bytes)

         For the lossy lanes the loss is documented and bounded — see
         the matrix below and
         [`spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md)."""]

    [body
      """The conformance suite includes round-trip fixtures for every
         supported projection; the runner shells out to
         `cx --from=… --to=…` in both directions and asserts byte
         equality (or documented-divergence) per format."""]

    [body
      """**Lossiness matrix** (reproduced from [`spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md) §9):"""]

    [table
      [tr [th 'from \\ to'] [th 'CX'] [th 'XML'] [th 'JSON'] [th 'YAML'] [th 'TOML'] [th 'MD'] [th 'CSV']]
      [tr [td 'CX'] [td 'lossless'] [td 'lossless‡'] [td 'lossy◊ / lossless▣'] [td 'lossy◊ / lossless▣'] [td 'lossy◊'] [td 'lossy◊'] [td 'lossy§']]
      [tr [td 'XML'] [td 'lossless'] [td 'lossless'] [td 'lossy◊'] [td 'lossy◊'] [td 'lossy◊'] [td 'lossy'] [td 'lossy§']]
      [tr [td 'JSON'] [td 'adds struct'] [td 'adds struct'] [td 'lossless¶'] [td 'lossless¶'] [td 'lossless¶'] [td 'lossy'] [td 'lossy§']]
      [tr [td 'YAML'] [td 'lossy\\*'] [td 'lossy\\*'] [td 'lossless¶'] [td 'lossless¶'] [td 'lossless¶'] [td 'lossy'] [td 'lossy§']]
      [tr [td 'TOML'] [td 'lossless\\*\\*'] [td 'lossless\\*\\*'] [td 'lossless¶'] [td 'lossless¶'] [td 'lossless¶'] [td 'lossy'] [td 'lossy§']]
      [tr [td 'MD'] [td 'lossy'] [td 'lossy'] [td 'lossy'] [td 'lossy'] [td 'lossy'] [td 'lossless'] [td 'lossy§']]
      [tr [td 'CSV'] [td 'lossy§'] [td 'lossy§'] [td 'lossy§'] [td 'lossy§'] [td 'lossy§'] [td 'lossy§'] [td 'lossless']]]

    [body
      """**Legend:**"""]
    [list
      [item """**lossless** — byte-identical round-trip within the
              format's expressive range."""]
      [item """**‡** — CX → XML is lossless when consumers preserve the
              `cx:` namespace attributes."""]
      [item """**¶** — lossless within the expressive range of that
              format."""]
      [item """**▣** — lossless under `--lossless`: the `$tag`
              structure envelope + value carriers
              ([`spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/conversions.md) §2.2.1 / §2.3.1)
              recover element documents byte-identically
              (strict-canonical eq). The default (idiomatic) lane keeps
              the ◊ losses. Program directives are outside every
              lane's lossless domain."""]
      [item """**◊** — CX → JSON/YAML/TOML/MD default lane with CXDM
              container Items (Array, Map, Sequence-as-Item):
              non-string Map keys are coerced to strings on emit
              (lossy); the Sequence-vs-Array distinction is lost
              (in lossless mode both recover, via the `cx:key-type`
              sidecar and the `cx:seq` carrier)."""]
      [item """**§** — Delimited (CSV/TSV/PSV) is well-defined but lossy:
              type metadata is lost on emit; anchors, aliases, and
              merges are resolved (an aliased row projects as a full
              row); comments, multi-document, mixed content, and PIs
              are stripped or error; element names are lost on parse
              (recovered via schema or auto-typing — see
              [[delim-types]])."""]
      [item """\\* — YAML anchors and aliases are resolved (expanded) on
              parse; not reconstructed in CX output. Data content is
              preserved."""]
      [item """\\*\\* — TOML has no mixed content or comments, so CX
              output contains only what TOML can express; no loss
              within TOML's range."""]]

    [body
      """**Practically:** you can store data as JSON for the JS
         ecosystem, convert to CX for selection / transformation /
         hashing, then convert back to lossless-lane JSON for delivery —
         no information loss, no re-formatting drift, no string-mangling
         in between. The CSV / TSV / Markdown projections are
         documented one-way exports for their respective ecosystems;
         round-trip back into CX recovers structure but not the
         lossy-on-emit metadata. The CXCol / Parquet bridges are
         lossless for table-shaped data and one-way for everything
         else."""]

    [body
      """The hash is taken over **strict canonical bytes** (see
         [[canonical-strict]]), not over the surface form. Two
         documents that round-trip identically through a lossless lane
         hash to the same SHA-256 — including across surfaces. This
         is the foundation of CX's content-addressing story (see
         [[hash]])."""]]]
